in the age of imperialism there were few  places in asia which got through without  coming under direct rule by a foreign  power  now in the middle east there was one  glaring exception iran who was never  conquered even briefly  but the question is why why was iran  never colonized  so as of the 19th century the borders of  iran or persia as it was known then  looked like this  it was ruled by the kajar dynasty whose  primary concern wasn't european powers  but the ottoman empire to its west  however as the ottomans declined two  more powers stepped into the void to  potentially threaten persia  the first was the russian empire whose  expansion eastward and southward brought  it into a series of conflict  persians by the mid 19th century the  russians had taken this territory and  was now a major trade partner of persia  and it was shortly after this that  persia came into contact with britain  who clashed with them and the qajar  dynasty lost control of these  economically important lands  now the rulers of persia at this point  weren't silly and they knew that persia  was declining and that the two powers  weren't going to stop until they had  control of persia in its entirety  persia's rulers knew that they couldn't  put up much resistance to either empire  in the event of an invasion so they did  the only thing that they could  play both britain and russia off against  each other and this did work for a time  but over the course of the late 19th  century britain's economic control  continued to grow  and thereafter something important  happened persia struck oil  a lot of oil which other nations wanted  britain's economic influence led to the  persian government signing overall oil  rights in the early 20th century to the  anglo-persian oil company  nowadays called bp 1907 also saw the  closest thing to the colonization of  persia  the anglo-russian convention in which  britain and russia carved the country up  into spheres of economic influence with  a little remaining free  thus persia's position as a state which  sat partially in both nations spheres of  influence kept it from formally being an  imperial domain of either  however when world war one rolled up it  did cause issues when the ottoman empire  declared war on the ontart persia being  somewhat aligned with them became a  target  the british and russians maintained  their zones of influence there and the  ottomans invaded and occupied some of  the eastern parts of the state  when the russian empire collapsed in the  wake of the bolshevik revolution its  forces left persia and the ottomans  would soon after go on to lose the war  and break apart  this left only britain who thanked  persia for its sacrifice and hard work  and made sure that as a result it would  be left independent forever more just  kidding  instead what happened was that britain  tried to make it into an unofficial  protectorate by enforcing its economic  hegemony there the ruler of persia at  this point ahmed shah agreed to these  terms because he didn't feel like dying  but they weren't implemented for very  long  this was because the us france and the  newly formed ussr decried the treaties  amounting to essentially annexation and  it was soon afterwards abandoned  and after this britain subsequently  withdrew leaving a somewhat independent  persia  thus iran did what many others failed to  do it survived the age of imperialism  and the reason why iran was never  conquered in its entirety was that it  played both britain and russia off  against each other  this meant that much of the country  would be under the effective economic  control of one of these two powers but  it did spur iran the same fate as places  like india or syria  which was something i hope you enjoyed  this episode and a special thanks to my  patreon supporters  james bissonette john lucid danny  maloney a man of culture  marvin castell john bisquez mo rob  waterhouse  james castaneda aaron the white jordan  longley gustav